1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Regarding his son's biography: "...The mother's name is unknown..."
2 CONT
2 CONT Egas Moniz I of Ribadouro, called The Gascon or The Old (c. 977 - before 1044) was a medieval nobleman of the County of Portugal, having been lord of Ribadouro, governor of Lamego and the founder of the Monastery of Cucujães.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Egas was the eldest son of Moninho Viegas, o Gasco,[1] who is generally considered the founder of the House of Ribadouro. His mother's name is unknown. From his patronymic, it is possible to say that his paternal grandfather was c
2 CONC alled Egas, probably Egas Moniz, a name that would become quite common in the family (in fact, it was the name of his eldest brother and heir to the house). From his father he inherited the epithet The Gascon, common in the family
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Old accounts tell that in 999, in a year of change in the kingdom of León, when Bermudo II died and Count Mendo Gonçalves of Portugal became regent of the young Afonso V of León, a landing of Christians took place at the mouth of 
2 CONC the Douro, commanded by Monio Viegas, the supposed founder of the Ribaduriense (or Gascan) lineage, who was said to have originated in Gascony.[2]  This information may be credible, but in a donation that Garcia Moniz made in 1068
2 CONC  to the King of Galicia, it refers to goods that he had inherited from his grandparents, so that these, the supposed parents of the “founder from Gascony” already had control in Ribadouro, and so Monio did not conquer such control
2 CONC , but inherited it, and therefore could not come from Gascony, as he was a native of Portugal, probably from a Portuguese place called Gasconha (or Casconha in the current municipality of Paredes).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Egas inherited several assets in Ribadouro from his father, especially in the regions of Penafiel (Galegos, Canelas, etc.) and Castelo de Paiva (Pedorido, Raiva, etc.).[1]  He also exercised authority in these same lands, probably
2 CONC  conferred by Afonso V of León and Bermudo III of León.[1]  
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Toda Ermiges da Maia, who, in addition to being a first cousin of the first lord of Maia, Trastamiro Aboazar, was the maternal granddaughter of Trutesendo Galindes, from the influential lineage of the founders of Paço d
2 CONC e Sousa Monastery.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT It is known that he was already deceased in 1044.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and descendants
2 CONT Egas married Toda Ermiges da Maia, daughter of Ermígio Aboazar da Maia and his wife, Vivilide Trutesendes. The marriage resulted in the following descendants:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ermígio Viegas I of Ribadouro ( 1020 -?), lord of Ribadouro, married Unisco Pais.[3][4]
2 CONT Monio Viegas II of Ribadouro (before 1044-before 1097), married Unisco Trastamires.
2 CONT Pedro Viegas de Ribadouro (before 1044-after 1070), married Sancha Pinioliz, and in second marriage Toda Pais.
2 CONT Unisco Viegas of Ribadouro (d. before 1118), married Egas Gondesendes II of Baião.
2 CONT Énego Viegas II (fl. 1044).
2 CONT Gomes Viegas I (before 1044-before 1071).
2 CONT Vivilide Viegas (before 1044-after 1080), married Fernando Jeremias.[5][6]
2 CONT See also
2 CONT Moninho Viegas, o Gasco
2 CONT Garcia Moniz, o Gasco
2 CONT References
