0 @N6000000002187840503@ NOTE Eudes I, Duke of Burgundy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eudes I of Burgundy, surnamed Borel and called the Red, (1058–23 March 1103) was duke of Burgundy between 1079 and 1103. Eudes was the second son of Henry of Burgundy and Sybille of Barcelona. He became Duke of Burgundy following 
1 CONC the abdication of his older brother, Hugh I, who retired to become a Benedictine monk. Eudes married Maud of Burgundy (1065 - 1101), daughter of William I, Count of Burgundy and Stephanie de Longwy.
1 CONT
1 CONT An interesting incident is reported of this robber baron by an eyewitness Eadmer, biographer of Anselm of Canterbury. While Saint Anselm was progressing through Eudes's territory on his way to Rome in 1097, the bandit, expecting g
1 CONC reat treasure in the archbishop's retinue, prepared to ambush and loot it. Coming upon the prelate's train, the duke asked for the archbishop, who they had not found. Anselm promptly came forward and took the duke by surprise, say
1 CONC ing "My lord duke, suffer me to embrace thee." The flabbergasted duke immediately suffered the bishop to embrace him and offered himself as Anselm's humble servant.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a participant in the ill-fated Crusade of 1101.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT Gwatkin, H.M. Whitney, J.P. (ed) The Cambridge Medieval History: Volume II—The Rise of the Saracens and the Foundations of the Western Empire. Cambridge University Press, 1926. 
1 CONT See also: Dukes of Burgundy family tree
1 CONT
