0 @N6000000002188378527@ NOTE John II of Portugal
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John II
1 CONT King of Portugal
1 CONT and the Algarves
1 CONT of either side of the sea in Africa (more...)
1 CONT 17th century painting of John II
1 CONT 17th century painting of John II
1 CONT Reign November 11, 1477—November 15, 1477
1 CONT Predecessor Afonso V
1 CONT Successor Manuel I
1 CONT Consort Leonor of Viseu
1 CONT among others...
1 CONT Issue
1 CONT Afonso, Prince of Portugal
1 CONT Royal house House of Aviz
1 CONT Father Afonso V
1 CONT Mother Isabel of Coimbra
1 CONT Born March 3, 1455
1 CONT Alcáçovas Palace, Castle of São Jorge, Lisbon, Kingdom of Portugal
1 CONT Died October 25, 1495 (aged 40)
1 CONT Alvor, Portimão, Kingdom of Algarve
1 CONT Burial Monastery of Batalha, Batalha, District of Leiria, Portugal
1 CONT Portuguese royalty
1 CONT House of Avis
1 CONT
1 CONT John I
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Infante Duarte (future Edward I)
1 CONT    Infante Pedro, Duke of Coimbra
1 CONT    Henry the Navigator (Infante Henrique, Duke of Viseu)
1 CONT    Infanta Isabel, Duchess of Burgundy
1 CONT    Infante João, Lord of Reguengos
1 CONT    Infante Fernando, the Saint Prince
1 CONT    Afonso, Duke of Braganza (illegitimate)
1 CONT    Beatriz, Countess of Arundel (illegitimate)
1 CONT Grandchildren include
1 CONT    Infanta Isabel of Coimbra, Queen of Portugal
1 CONT Edward
1 CONT Children
1 CONT    Afonso, Prince of Portugal (future Afonso V)
1 CONT    Infante Fernando, Duke of Viseu
1 CONT    Infanta Leonor, Holy Roman Empress
1 CONT    Infanta Catarina
1 CONT    Infanta Joana, Queen of Castile
1 CONT Grandchildren include
1 CONT    Infante Manuel, Duke of Beja (future Manuel I)
1 CONT    Infanta Leonor of Viseu, Queen of Portugal
1 CONT Great-Grandchildren include
1 CONT    Jaime, Duke of Braganza, Prince of Portugal
1 CONT Afonso V
1 CONT Children include
1 CONT    João, Prince of Portugal
1 CONT    Blessed Joana, Princess of Portugal
1 CONT    João, Prince of Portugal (future John II)
1 CONT John II
1 CONT    Afonso, Prince of Portugal
1 CONT    Jorge, Duke of Coimbra (illegitimate)
1 CONT
1 CONT João II (Portuguese, pronounced [?u'?~u]; English: John II) (March 3, 1455 – October 25, 1495), the Perfect Prince (Port. o Príncipe Perfeito), was the thirteenth king of Portugal and the Algarves. He was born in Lisbon, the son o
1 CONC f king Afonso V of Portugal by his wife, Isabel of Coimbra, princess of Portugal. John II succeeded his father briefly in 1477 when the king retired to a monastery, but only became king in 1481.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a prince, John II accompanied his father in the campaigns in northern Africa and was made a knight by him after the victory in Arzila in 1471. In 1473 he married Leonor of Viseu, Infanta of Portugal and his first cousin.
1 CONT
1 CONT Even at a young age, he was not popular among the peers of the kingdom since he was immune to external influence and appeared to despise intrigue. The nobles (including particularly Fernando II, the Duke of Braganza) were afraid o
1 CONC f his future policies as king. Events proved them right.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the official accession to the throne in 1481, John II took a series of measures to curtail the overgrown power of his aristocracy and to concentrate power on himself. Immediately, the nobles started to conspire; John II did 
1 CONC nothing but observe. Letters of complaint and pleas to intervene were exchanged between the Duke of Braganza and Queen Isabella I of Castile. In 1483, this correspondence was intercepted by royal spies. The House of Braganza was o
1 CONC utlawed, their lands confiscated and the duke executed in Évora.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the following year, the Duke of Viseu, his cousin and brother-in-law was summoned to the palace and stabbed to death by the king himself for suspicion of a new conspiracy. Many other people were executed, murdered or exiled to 
1 CONC Castile including the bishop of Evora who was poisoned in prison.
1 CONT
1 CONT The king is reported to have said, concerning the rebellious nobles: "I'm the lord of lords, not the server of servants". After these events, no one in the country dared to defy the king. John II was free to govern as he pleased w
1 CONC ithout any other conspiracies during his reign. The nobles who sided with John II or surrendered were forced to make public pledges of loyalty, in return they were given certain privileges, yet they still had to pay taxes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Facing a bankrupt Kingdom, John II showed the initiative to solve the situation by creating an agile regime in which the Council of Scholars took a vital role. The king then made a background check on the population and selected m
1 CONC embers of the Council according to their abilities, talents and credentials. Popular complaints on judicial acts normally had the sympathy of the king. Already before the Tordesilhas Treaty, such was the profit coming from John II
1 CONC 's investements in the overseas explorations and expansion that the Portuguese currency had become the soundest in Europe. The Kingdom could finally collect taxes on its own as all of its debts had been paid of mainly thanks to it
1 CONC s main gold source at that time, the coast of Guinea.
1 CONT
1 CONT John II then restored the policies of Atlantic exploration, reviving the work of his great-uncle, Henry the Navigator. The Portuguese explorations were his main priority in government, pushing south the known coast of Africa with 
1 CONC the purpose of discovering the maritime route to India. During his reign, the following was achieved:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1482 - The first European settlement outside of Europe is founded, the coastal fortress and trade post of S. Jorge da Mina.
1 CONT     * 1484 – Diogo Cão discovered the Congo River
1 CONT     * 1488 - Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope
1 CONT     * 1493 – Álvaro Caminha started the settlement of the São Tomé and Príncipe islands
1 CONT     * Land expeditions were sent to India and Ethiopia in search of Prester John
1 CONT
1 CONT The complete extent of Portuguese voyages of exploration during this period is unknown. Much was kept secret for fear of competition with neighbouring Castile. The archives of this period were destroyed in the fire after the 1755 
1 CONC Lisbon earthquake and what was not destroyed during the earthquake was either stolen or destroyed during the Peninsular Wars and/or by pure negligence. Modern historians still debate their true extent, and some suspect that Portug
1 CONC uese sailors reached the continent of North America earlier than Christopher Columbus (by approximately 1470) and Brazil by as early as 1480 Template:Factdate.
1 CONT
1 CONT Arguments for this are the much more precise calculations on the diameter of the Earth that Portugal made (calculations from Arabic sources). Alongside John II was the Council of Scholars, composed of prominent scholars, mathemati
1 CONC cians, cartographers, theologians, high ranking religious figures of the Kingdom of Portugal, bankers and prominent Jewish intellectuals. These would later be known as the "School of Sagres". While Columbus thought it would be pos
1 CONC sible to reach India through the West, Portuguese intelligence knew already the way to be much longer and possibly that there was a continent in the middle Template:Factdate. The travels of the mysterious captain Duarte Pacheco Pe
1 CONC reira in the central Atlantic west of Cape Verde probably are more important than traditional history states. In fact a discovery which well reflects the lack of information and sources concerning the exploration efforts was the f
1 CONC inding of the order book of a bakery, close to Lisbon's Restelo shipyard which had an order for supplying biscuits for more than a hundred long voyages (east and west) between 1490 and 1497 Template:Factdate. This comes in direct 
1 CONC conflict with the traditional view that there were no voyages between the return of Bartolomeu Dias in 1488 and the departure of Vasco da Gama in 1497.
1 CONT
1 CONT While no direct proof exists, many historians[who?], both Portuguese and non-Portuguese suspect that the Americas may have already been explored and in the first steps of colonization. Not only concerning Brazil, but also Newfound
1 CONC land and Labrador, which ironically, despite being less known by the masses, are considered more plausible than those of Brazil. Among the several possible indications that could support the possible pre-Columbian exploration of B
1 CONC razil are:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. The lack of information, presumed due to Portuguese secrecy, surrounding these voyages;
1 CONT    2. The fact that the navigational routes the ships followed in order to pass the Cape of Good Hope by as early as the 1480s came to within 50 to 70 land miles of the coast of Brazil;
1 CONT    3. The agreed boundary made in the Treaty of Tordesillas divided South America more or less in half and reports during the talks mentioned John II's desire to give a precise position for this line (370 leagues) and not any "rou
1 CONC nd" measures.
1 CONT
1 CONT John II of Portugal
1 CONT
1 CONT When Columbus returned from his voyage he thought of first stopping by in Lisbon in order to claim his victory in front of King John II. King John II's only response to this was that under the treaty with Spain Columbus's discover
1 CONC ies lay within Portugal's sphere of influence. Before Columbus even reached Isabella of Castile, John II had already sent a letter to them threatening to send a fleet to claim it for Portugal. Spain quickly hastened to the negotia
1 CONC ting table which took place in a small town near the Portuguese border named Tordesillas. There was also a papal representative during that occasion in order to act as mediator. The result of this would be the famous Treaty of Tor
1 CONC desillas.
1 CONT
1 CONT But the dividing of the world was not the main issue between the Iberian kingdoms. Isabella I of Castile and Ferdinand II of Aragon had several daughters, but only one feeble male heir — Juan. The oldest daughter, Isabella of Arag
1 CONC on, was married to Prince Afonso of Portugal since childhood. Afonso was John II's only son and beloved by the king. If Juan died without male heir, as was probable, Afonso would be heir not only of Portugal but also of Castile an
1 CONC d Aragon. This threat to Castilian and Aragonese independence was very real and the Catholic kings tried every diplomatic trick to dissolve the wedding. Finally, in 1491, Afonso died in mysterious circumstances — a fall from a hor
1 CONC se during a ride in the margin of the Tagus river. The influence of the Catholic kings in this accident was never proved but the prince was an excellent rider, his Castilian valet fled never to be seen again and after this, Isabel
1 CONC la, the heiress, was no longer married to the enemy. John tried without success until the end of his life to legitimise Jorge, Duke of Coimbra, his illegitimate son.
1 CONT
1 CONT John II died at Alvor without leaving male issue. Because of the hatred the Portuguese nobility had for him, the hypothesis of poisoning was never ruled out. He was succeeded by his first cousin Manuel I.
1 CONT
1 CONT The nickname the Perfect Prince is a late description and refers to Niccolò Machiavelli's work The Prince. John II is considered to have lived his life exactly according to the writer's idea of a perfect prince. To his contemporar
1 CONC ies, John II was known as the Tyrant.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Page, Martin The First Global Village
1 CONT     * Boxer, Charles R. From Lisbon to Goa, 1500-1750 (1991)
1 CONT     * Boxer, Charles R. The Portuguese Seaborne Empire 1415-1825
1 CONT     * Mira, Manuel S. Forgotten Portuguese: The Melungeons and the Portuguese Making of America (1998)
1 CONT     * Duffy, James Portuguese Africa (1968)
1 CONT     * Bodian, Mirian Hebrews of the Portuguese Nation (1997)
1 CONT
